          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"“I love you.” (Already in English.)\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"Goneril directly presents her love for Lear as true and amplifies it through comparisons. This is a clear avowal, regardless of whether it is sincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Beyond all manner of so much I love you.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The focal expression asserts the extent of Goneril’s present love but does not promise future conduct, renew a commitment, or undertake an obligation. Lear’s subsequent grant is his action, not an undertaking contained in her avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs within an elaborate, controlled answer to Lear’s formal question, not as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its intensity and hyperbole do not by themselves establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Our eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.78,\"reason\":\"The avowal also weakly functions as a competitive, courtly answer in a public test tied to material bounty: Goneril claims to love Lear most and rhetorically magnifies his value. This residual function may be described as praise or flattery, without deciding that it is insincere.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\\nThat we our largest bounty may extend\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Dearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a formal court setting, Lear asks his daughters to state which loves him most, explicitly connecting their answers with the distribution of his kingdom. Goneril, commanded to speak first, addresses the focal expression to her father as part of an expansive declaration of love.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Goneril makes no identifiable promise or relational undertaking in the focal expression; she avows and ranks her present love. Lear rewards her answer by granting territory, but that response does not convert her avowal into a commitment. The supplied evidence does not settle her sincerity.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied title and text identify the work as William Shakespeare’s The Tragedy of King Lear. The classification relies only on the supplied passages.\",\"limitations\":\"The target is identified as the exact substring in Goneril’s speech. The excerpt establishes the avowal’s public and competitive setting but does not independently establish Goneril’s actual affection, sincerity, private intention, or later conduct.\"}"
